#
# Rules to create the native Emu68 m68k target.
#

include $(SRCDIR)/config/aros.cfg

#
# AROS-<target> is the whole distribution, not just the ELF.
#
# It used to be declared as depending on kernel-link alone, which made
# `make AROS-emu68-m68k` a slow way of rebuilding the same ELF and left the
# distribution tree without S/, Fonts/, Storage/, Utilities/, Tools/ or
# AROS.boot. The SD card was therefore always made from a foreign tree, and
# every module on it -- libraries, Zune classes, the commands in C: -- came
# from there rather than from this build. A patch touching module code changed
# nothing that booted, silently. See AI_context/consolidated/history/ISSUE-0007.md.
#
# Shaped after arch/m68k-amiga, minus the Amiga-specific packaging and demos.
#
# compiler-posixc, compiler-stdcio and external-openurl-lib are named
# explicitly because they hang off the generic `AROS` metatarget rather than
# off AROS-<target>, so `workbench-complete workbench` does not reach them.
# Their absence is not a build error: the tree simply comes out with 59
# libraries instead of 62, and the boot stops waiting for posixc.library.
#
#MM- AROS-emu68-m68k : kernel-link-emu68-m68k software-emu68-m68k
#MM- AROS-emu68-m68k-quick : kernel-link-emu68-m68k-quick

# workbench-complete selects the platform USB stack through
# kernel-usb-$(AROS_TARGET_ARCH)-$(AROS_TARGET_CPU).  Binding that generic name
# to Poseidon's disk-based, non-PCI stack and Bellatrix's native DWC2 host
# driver is what puts USB on the card; without the alias a distribution
# silently contains neither side.
#
# USB IS ENABLED (2026-08-29).
#
# It was commented out on 2026-08-22 so that a host controller owning
# interrupts and DMA was not in the picture while the heap (ISSUE-0037) and the
# first GL workload (ISSUE-0045) were being chased. That is over, and the cost
# of leaving it off is a distribution with no keyboard and no mouse -- which is
# not a state anyone asks for on purpose, and which nothing in the build says
# out loud.
#
# The driver named here is dwc2emu68, the Bellatrix rewrite (ISSUE-0047), not
# the inherited arm-native engine it replaces. Both are still built; this line
# is what decides which one a distribution contains.
#
# Disabling is commenting the line out again. It cannot be a make variable:
# mmake reads #MM lines as text when it scans the mmakefiles, so a
# $(USB_BACKEND) here would be a metatarget with that literal name, which does
# not exist and which mmake drops without a word.
#
# The Startup-Sequence needs no change either way. Its USB block is guarded on
# DEVS:USBHardware/dwc2emu68.device existing.
#
# THE DRIVER ON THE CARD IS usb2otg, AND THAT CHANGED ON 2026-08-30.
#
# dwc2emu68 is the Bellatrix rewrite (ISSUE-0047) and it was the default all
# day. It is not, today, good enough to be the machine's only USB: with it the
# boot stops about eight seconds in and the desktop is never reached. Two
# defects were found and fixed in it during that day -- a NAK retry that
# re-armed a channel the core had not released, and a handler that never
# cleared the core interrupts it does not dispatch, which storms the m68k
# until no task is ever scheduled again -- and a third is still open: a
# control SETUP to a device behind the hub completes and its data stage is
# never armed.
#
# usb2otg is the AROS engine this port inherited. Swapping it in was the
# experiment that separated "the machine is broken" from "our driver is
# broken", and with it the machine boots and the mouse works. It answers a
# question ours cannot yet: what correct looks like on this silicon.
#
# So it takes the card while ours is finished. Both still build -- that is the
# whole reason two exist -- and switching back is this line.
#
#MM- kernel-usb-m68k-emu68 : kernel-usb-nopci kernel-usb-arosotg

# rom/ and workbench/ select the Bluetooth stack the same way, through
# kernel-bluetooth-$(AROS_TARGET_ARCH)-$(AROS_TARGET_CPU), and this target
# defined no such alias -- so AROS's Bluetooth has never been built here at
# all. That is what makes the bluzing submodule look necessary: the card
# carries a bluetooth.library from Extras/aros-bluzing because nothing was
# putting AROS's own there.
#
# AROS HEAD now ships the stack itself (rom/bluetooth, licensed
# LICENSE.aros-bluzing -- it is the same code, imported), so the alias is what
# turns it on. What is still missing afterwards is the transport: the stack
# talks to HCI devices in DEVS:Bluetooth, and the only one upstream is vbthci,
# a virtual one. The Pi's onboard controller hangs off the PL011 that
# soc/bluetooth/pl011bt.resource already owns, and nothing yet bridges the two.
# See ISSUE-0046.
# The components of kernel-bluetooth, minus the firmware loaders.
#
# kernel-bluetooth pulls kernel-bluetooth-firmware, and rom/bluetooth/firmware
# holds exactly two loaders, both Realtek. This board's radio is a Broadcom
# BCM43438 on the PL011, and BTStackLoader calls bLoadFirmwareLoaders()
# unconditionally -- so every card carried two modules that can never match the
# hardware, and on a real Pi they surface in the stack as Realtek parts that
# are not there.
#
# The chip does not need them: a BCM43438 runs from ROM, answers HCI_Reset and
# reports its BD_ADDR with no patchram at all. Firmware adds fixes and
# features; it is not a precondition for the controller working.
#
# Listing the parts rather than the aggregate keeps this out of upstream's
# mmakefile, where an ifeq would not work anyway -- mmake reads #MM lines as
# text when it scans, so a conditional around them is not seen. When a
# Broadcom loader exists, kernel-bluetooth-firmware goes back on this line.
# See ISSUE-0053.
#MM- kernel-bluetooth-m68k-emu68 : \
#MM      kernel-bluetooth-library \
#MM      kernel-bluetooth-classes \
#MM      bluetooth-shellcommands \
#MM      kernel-bluetooth-hardware \
#MM      kernel-bluetooth-prefs \
#MM      kernel-h4bthci \
#MM      kernel-brcmbt


#
# `contrib` is on the list because nothing else puts it there. Upstream expects
# the AROS contrib repository to be checked out into contrib/ and reached by an
# AROS-complete-<target> rule that this port does not have; ours is a symlink to
# aros/contrib in this repository, and its mmakefiles declare `#MM- contrib :`
# hooks that were being collected and never built. The symptom was a full build
# that produced a distribution with an Extras/ package missing its
# S/Package-Startup and its Prefs/Env-Archive/SYS/Packages registration, so the
# package existed on the card and never installed its assigns.
#
#MM- software-emu68-m68k : general-setup boot workbench-emu68-m68k \
#MM                         workbench-c-emu68-bootprogress \
#MM                         compiler-posixc compiler-stdcio external-openurl-lib \
#MM                         kernel-m68k-emu68-vcgfx hidd-vc4gallium contrib contrib-bellatrix
#MM- workbench-emu68-m68k : workbench-complete workbench

#MM- distfiles-emu68-m68k : AROS-emu68-m68k
#MM- distfiles-emu68-m68k-quick : AROS-emu68-m68k-quick

#
# WiFi firmware for bwfm.device, fetched the way arch/arm-raspi already does.
#
# The chip has no firmware of its own: bwfm.device loads
# DEVS:Firmware/brcmfmac<id>-sdio.bin and its .txt NVRAM at runtime, and
# without them the driver attaches and associates with nothing. This target
# is a copy of arm-raspi's distfiles-raspi-wifi-fw, narrowed to the one chip
# this board has -- the BCM43430 on a Pi 3 -- rather than every Pi's.
#
# The branch matters and is not a version preference: "buster" keeps real
# files under brcm/, while newer branches symlink them to cypress/ and
# GitHub's ?raw= would hand back the link text instead of the blob. That is
# arm-raspi's finding, kept here with it.
#
# Fetched rather than vendored: the blobs are ~400 KB and redistributable
# under LICENCE.broadcom_bcm43xx, which comes along for that reason.
#
EMU68WIFIFW_BRANCH := buster
EMU68WIFIFW_URI    := https://github.com/RPi-Distro/firmware-nonfree/blob/$(EMU68WIFIFW_BRANCH)
EMU68WIFIFW_FILES  := brcm/brcmfmac43430-sdio.bin brcm/brcmfmac43430-sdio.txt \
                     LICENCE.broadcom_bcm43xx

#
# Bluetooth patchram for the onboard controller.
#
# A BCM43438 runs from ROM until it is sent a patchram image: it answers
# HCI_Reset and reports its version without one, but with a placeholder
# BD_ADDR of AA:AA:AA:AA:AA:AA, and pairing needs a real address.
#
# soc/bluetooth/brcmbt reads this at runtime from DEVS:Firmware/brcm/. The
# legacy tree fetched the same file and converted it to a C source at build
# time (BTstack's convert_hcd.py) to link it in; keeping it a file means
# changing controllers is changing a file.
#
# Same source the legacy build used. BCM43430A1 is the Pi 3 and Zero W part.
#
EMU68BTFW_DEVICE := BCM43430A1
EMU68BTFW_URI    := https://github.com/OpenELEC/misc-firmware/raw/master/firmware/brcm

#MM
distfiles-emu68-bt-fw:
	@$(MKDIR) $(AROSDIR)/Devs/Firmware/brcm
	@wget -t 5 -T 15 -c "$(EMU68BTFW_URI)/$(EMU68BTFW_DEVICE).hcd" \
	    -O "$(AROSDIR)/Devs/Firmware/brcm/$(EMU68BTFW_DEVICE).hcd" \
	    || $(ECHO) "WARNING: could not fetch Bluetooth firmware $(EMU68BTFW_DEVICE).hcd"

#MM
distfiles-emu68-wifi-fw:
	@$(MKDIR) $(AROSDIR)/Devs/Firmware
	@for f in $(EMU68WIFIFW_FILES) ; do \
	    wget -t 5 -T 15 -c "$(EMU68WIFIFW_URI)/$$f?raw=true" -O "$(AROSDIR)/Devs/Firmware/$${f##*/}" \
	        || $(ECHO) "WARNING: could not fetch WiFi firmware $$f" ; \
	done

#
# The WirelessManager configuration, which nothing else creates.
#
# It is not firmware and it is not fetched -- it is a file this port has to put
# on the card, because the only writer of it is Prefs/Network and that writer
# refuses to run before a network exists (`if (prefs.networkCount > 0)`,
# workbench/prefs/network/prefsdata.c). Without the file C:WirelessManager
# aborts before opening its window, so the scanner that would find the first
# network is unreachable until someone types one in by hand. See the header of
# soc/wifi/Wireless.prefs.
#
# ENVARC: and not ENV:, because ENV: is RAM:ENV and does not survive a reboot;
# S:Startup-Sequence copies ENVARC: into it on the way up.
#
#MM
distfiles-emu68-wifi-prefs:
	@$(MKDIR) $(AROS_ENVARC)/SYS
	@$(CP) $(SRCDIR)/arch/m68k-emu68/soc/wifi/Wireless.prefs \
	    $(AROS_ENVARC)/SYS/Wireless.prefs

#
# Persistent first-boot network configuration for the onboard WiFi.
#
# Wireless.prefs lets WirelessManager parse a configuration, but does not
# cause AROSTCP's Package-Startup to run it.  Prefs/Network normally creates
# these variables and database files when the user saves an interface.  Seed
# the same state in ENVARC so a release image starts bwfm.device unit 0 and
# displays WirelessManager's AppIcon before any preferences have been saved.
#
# Keep AROSTCP's static databases upstream-owned.  The five files copied below
# are the same ones CopyDefaultConfiguration() installs in Prefs/Network; the
# emu68 port owns only the generated first-boot values.
#
EMU68_AROSTCP_DEFAULT_FILES := inet.access netdb networks protocols services

#MM
distfiles-emu68-wifi-network-prefs:
	@$(MKDIR) $(AROS_ENVARC)/AROSTCP/db
	@for f in $(EMU68_AROSTCP_DEFAULT_FILES) ; do \
	    $(CP) $(AROSDIR)/System/Network/AROSTCP/db/$$f \
	        $(AROS_ENVARC)/AROSTCP/db/$$f ; \
	done
	@$(CP) $(SRCDIR)/arch/m68k-emu68/soc/wifi/arostcp/AutoRun \
	    $(AROS_ENVARC)/AROSTCP/AutoRun
	@$(CP) $(SRCDIR)/arch/m68k-emu68/soc/wifi/arostcp/WirelessAutoRun \
	    $(AROS_ENVARC)/AROSTCP/WirelessAutoRun
	@$(CP) $(SRCDIR)/arch/m68k-emu68/soc/wifi/arostcp/WirelessDevice \
	    $(AROS_ENVARC)/AROSTCP/WirelessDevice
	@$(CP) $(SRCDIR)/arch/m68k-emu68/soc/wifi/arostcp/Config \
	    $(AROS_ENVARC)/AROSTCP/Config
	@$(CP) $(SRCDIR)/arch/m68k-emu68/soc/wifi/arostcp/MobileAutorun \
	    $(AROS_ENVARC)/AROSTCP/MobileAutorun
	@$(CP) $(SRCDIR)/arch/m68k-emu68/soc/wifi/arostcp/db/general.config \
	    $(AROS_ENVARC)/AROSTCP/db/general.config
	@$(CP) $(SRCDIR)/arch/m68k-emu68/soc/wifi/arostcp/db/interfaces \
	    $(AROS_ENVARC)/AROSTCP/db/interfaces
	@$(CP) $(SRCDIR)/arch/m68k-emu68/soc/wifi/arostcp/db/hosts \
	    $(AROS_ENVARC)/AROSTCP/db/hosts
	@$(CP) $(SRCDIR)/arch/m68k-emu68/soc/wifi/arostcp/db/netdb-myhost \
	    $(AROS_ENVARC)/AROSTCP/db/netdb-myhost
	@$(CP) $(SRCDIR)/arch/m68k-emu68/soc/wifi/arostcp/db/static-routes \
	    $(AROS_ENVARC)/AROSTCP/db/static-routes
